,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-06","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-17","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-13","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Rescheduled","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-14","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Income dependency high at 31.7% of service costs recovered from fees and charges, above peer councils, creating vulnerability to demand or collection failure","lead":"Councils that are unusually dependent on discretionary income (as opposed to council tax or central grants) face steeper revenue cuts if the economy weakens or behaviour changes. The council's higher recovery rate is an asset but also a vulnerability in\u2026","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Large upfront capital programme of \u00a329.2m in 2026/27, heavily weighted to cinema development, must be delivered within tightening revenue budget","lead":"A large capital programme frontloaded into a year of tight revenue is a structural vulnerability. If capital costs overrun or schemes are delayed, the council must either find revenue to cover financing costs or reduce service spending. The cinema is\u2026","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Government letter on Local Government Reorganisation creates structural uncertainty about future baseline funding and form","lead":"LGR creates a planning horizon problem: the council must budget as if it will continue to exist and must balance its books, but may be substantially restructured. This affects both the council's ability to deliver long-term plans and a lender's view of\u2026","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","headline":"Income dependency high at 31.7% of service costs recovered from fees and charges, above peer councils, creating vulnerability to demand or collection failure","detail":"Lichfield recovers 31.7% of service expenditure from sales, fees and charges compared to 21.5% for nearest neighbours and 24.1% for English district councils. This income recovery has grown 23.5% since 2023/24. While this shows good self-sufficiency, it also means the council is more vulnerable than peers to volume falls (if residents reduce garden waste subscriptions or car park usage, or if the cinema does not attract expected footfall) or collection failure.","category":"income","matters":"Councils that are unusually dependent on discretionary income (as opposed to council tax or central grants) face steeper revenue cuts if the economy weakens or behaviour changes. The council's higher recovery rate is an asset but also a vulnerability in downturns.","figures":"Sales, fees and charges as proportion of service expenditure: 31.7% (Lichfield) vs 21.5% (Nearest Neighbours) vs 24.1% (English District Councils). Growth 2023/24 to 2024/25: 23.5% (Lichfield) vs 19.1% (NN) vs 15.3% (England).","quote":"Overall, the Council recovered 31.7% of service expenditure from sales, fees, and charges and this was higher than 21.5% for Nearest Statistical Neighbours and 24.1% for English District Councils.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","headline":"Large upfront capital programme of \u00a329.2m in 2026/27, heavily weighted to cinema development, must be delivered within tightening revenue budget","detail":"The capital programme commits \u00a329.2m in 2026/27 (\u00a39.3m being the cinema development), dropping to \u00a33.7m by 2029/30. This must be financed from internal borrowing (\u00a37.5m in 2026/27, then repaid), capital receipts, grants, and reserves. The paper notes that 'the risk around higher general inflation is increasing and construction inflation tends to be at a higher level than general inflation,' and the Capital Contingency budget of \u00a3350k may need review. If construction costs run over or schemes slip into years with tighter revenue, there will be pressure to either cut services or increase borrowing.","category":"capital","matters":"A large capital programme frontloaded into a year of tight revenue is a structural vulnerability. If capital costs overrun or schemes are delayed, the council must either find revenue to cover financing costs or reduce service spending. The cinema is discretionary investment and assumes \u00a3100-200k future income that is flagged as at risk.","figures":"Capital Expenditure 2026/27: \u00a329,167k (of which cinema \u00a39,324k). Capital Contingency: \u00a3350k. Cinema assumed income 2028/29: \u00a3100k; 2029/30 onwards: \u00a3200k.","quote":"The risk around higher general inflation is increasing and construction inflation tends to be at a higher level than general inflation. The level of the Capital Contingency Budget of \u00a3350,000 will also need to be reviewed in the current economic climate.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","headline":"Government letter on Local Government Reorganisation creates structural uncertainty about future baseline funding and form","detail":"The council states: 'The Council recently received a letter from the Government highlighting the importance of continuing to operate as a going concern with balanced budgets and adequate levels of reserves until such time as the legal framework for Local Government Reorganisation is implemented.' LGR is listed as a strategic risk affecting recruitment, retention, morale and service delivery. The timing, financial impact, and form of any merger are unknown. This uncertainty pervades the entire MTFS because the council does not know what its baseline will be post-reorganisation.","category":"governance","matters":"LGR creates a planning horizon problem: the council must budget as if it will continue to exist and must balance its books, but may be substantially restructured. This affects both the council's ability to deliver long-term plans and a lender's view of baseline risk. The Government's explicit call for 'going concern' budgets signals concerns at national level about local authority finances.","figures":"Strategic Risk SR1 (LGR) current score 12, within risk appetite but at edge. Likelihood and Impact both rated Amber.","quote":"There remains an inherently high level of uncertainty surrounding Local Government Reorganisation and its financial impact on the budget of this Council.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","headline":"Structural funding gap widens each year from \u00a350k to \u00a32,128k with no identified solution","detail":"The council's revenue funding falls short of expenditure by growing amounts: \u00a350k in 2027/28, \u00a3869k in 2028/29, \u00a32,128k in 2029/30, \u00a32,092k in 2030/31. These gaps are currently being closed by drawing down reserves. The council has identified the problem but not how it will be solved \u2014 only \u00a350k per year in 'Alternative Budget Proposals' has been identified. This means either major council tax rises, major service cuts, or both must be announced by early 2027 for 2027/28 but ideally now for 2028/29.","category":"savings","matters":"An MTFS that names the problem but not the solution is incomplete. A lender needs to see either concrete savings proposals or a credible path to tax rises. Without it, the council is in a holding pattern where the difficult budget conversation keeps deferring.","figures":"Funding Gap: \u00a350k (2027/28) \u2192 \u00a3869k (2028/29) \u2192 \u00a32,128k (2029/30) \u2192 \u00a32,092k (2030/31). Alternative Budget Proposals identified: \u00a350k per year.","quote":"Approved Funding Gap / (Transfer to Reserves) 0 0 50 869 2,128 2,092","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","headline":"General reserves depleted to near minimum level by 2030/31, breaching the council's own prudence floor","detail":"The council's available general reserves decline from \u00a33,264k in 2027/28 to \u00a3216k by 2030/31 under the approved MTFS. The minimum level is set at \u00a32,000k. By the final year, the council would be unable to fund unexpected events or smooth volatility without drawing below its own safety threshold. This is not a projection of 'what if' but the stated central case if no changes are made.","category":"reserves","matters":"Depleted reserves reduce borrowing headroom, increase vulnerability to demand shocks or income falls, and signal unsustainable budget position to rating agencies and lenders. Breach of the minimum could trigger intervention or restrictions on service delivery.","figures":"Available General Reserves: \u00a33,264k (2027/28) \u2192 \u00a3216k (2030/31); Minimum Level: \u00a32,000k","quote":"Available General Reserves Year End \u00a33,264 \u00a33,264 \u00a33,214 \u00a32,344 \u00a3216","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Lichfield","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Income dependency high at 31.7% of service costs recovered from fees and charges, above peer councils, creating vulnerability to demand or collection failure","lead":"Councils that are unusually dependent on discretionary income (as opposed to council tax or central grants) face steeper revenue cuts if the economy weakens or behaviour changes. The council's higher recovery rate is an asset but also a vulnerability in\u2026","also":["4 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-17","n_findings":5,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"MTFS Report FINAL","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.lichfielddc.gov.uk/documents/s21789/21789.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":41,"news_drafts_total":3}}